Z||000000||fiero56||07-19-2001||12:40 AM||||Most books I've found on chevy 60* v-6s recommend high-volume, high-pressure oil pumps. What is the typical pressure these high-pressure pumps put out on a rebuilt engine? I know there are safety margins, but wondering what these typically pump.<P>Any brand recomendations?<P>12:40am and I can't even spell oil right, time for more Dew!<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||07-19-2001||06:51 AM||||I have Melling HP, 80 pnds||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||07-19-2001||09:02 AM||||Unless you're rebuilding for high RPM, you don't need any more pressure than stock.<P>Just buy the high volume pump and put your OEM pressure spring into it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||07-19-2001||08:10 PM||||80psi is pretty safe for the engine? This engine is planned to be permanent. If anything else goes in the Formula, I will trans-plant the engine to my other Fiero, so I have plans on it lasting at least another 13yrs and 180K miles.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-20-2001||10:17 AM||||Here's the deal: 80 psi of oil pressure is entirely unnecessary for a street engine.<BR>Oil pressure is required to push the oil from the crankshaft main bearings into the crankshaft to go down the drilled passages in the crank to get to the rod bearings. The oil only has to have enough pressure to overcome centrifugal force as it goes from main bearing in toward the center of the crankshaft. Once it passes the center of the crank, centrifugal force pushes it out toward the rod bearing.<BR>The centrifugal force the oil has to overcome to get into the crankshaft is entirely a function of crank RPM.<P>If you will not be spinning this engine to more than 6500 RPM, you do not need any more oil pressure than stock. Just use the stock pressure regulator spring with the high volume pump.<P>More pressure than necessary is just parasitic loss on the engine. The energy from the parasitic loss goes into unnecessary heating of the oil.<P>80 psi may sound cool, but it is way more than a street engine needs and is in fact wasteful on a street engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||07-20-2001||01:00 PM||||all true. but i shift mine at usually 6,000 and sometimes get up to low 7's (limiter is set for 7300 but I had it to 7500 a couple times before i installed it). Its ran fine for 86,000 miles now, and still not a single noise, doesnt burn any oil between changes (usually 5k-7k).<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
